    "answer": "The national operational standard for processing a KITAS extension is estimated at **20 to 30 working days**, which translates to approximately 3 to 5 weeks [1, 2]. This timeline is administratively divided into initial document verification (3 to 5 working days), main processing and verification checks (10 to 15 working days), and the final issuance after approval (5 to 7 working days) [2].\n\nFrom a strictly normative standpoint, as mandated by **Permenkumham Nomor 11 Tahun 2024 (Pasal 94A Ayat 5)**, you must submit the extension application and pay the required immigration fees before your current permit expires [3, 4]. If this legal requirement is met, you are legally protected and will not be classified as overstaying, even if the government's processing time extends beyond your KITAS expiration date [3-5]. \n\nRegarding the fees, it is critical to distinguish between the national statutory tariffs and local service rates in Bali:\n\n*   **National Government Fee (PNBP):** Under **Peraturan Pemerintah (PP) Nomor 45 Tahun 2024**, which dictates the Non-Tax State Revenues (PNBP) for the Ministry of Law and Human Rights, the official state fee for a 1-year Limited Stay Permit (Izin Tinggal Terbatas) is set at **Rp 3.000.000** per application [6]. \n*   **Local Bali Agency Fees:** If you utilize a local immigration agency in Bali to process a working KITAS (E23) renewal, the commercial rate includes the national government fees as well as the professional service fees required to renew the associated work authorization (RPTKA). Current operational pricing in Bali places the total cost for an E23 KITAS renewal between **Rp 14.000.000 and Rp 20.000.000**, depending on the specific provider [1, 7].\n\nBoth the processing timeline and the agency fees apply to standard renewals; express processing may be available locally at a 30-50% premium, though this depends strictly on the workload of the local Immigration Office (Kantor Imigrasi) at the time of appl