    "answer": "Based on the provided sources, I do not have any specific information or legal references mentioning the \"HGB\" (Hak Guna Bangunan) title in relation to KITAS holders; therefore, I cannot speculate on the eligibility for this specific property title.\n\nHowever, the regulatory framework clearly defines the general property ownership restrictions for foreigners in Indonesia. Foreign nationals, including KITAS holders (such as those applying for the Second Home Visa E33A), are only permitted to access \"strata title\" under the Right of Use, legally defined as **Hak Pakai** [1, 2]. The absolute Right of Ownership, known as **Hak Milik**, is strictly prohibited for foreigners and is exclusively reserved for Indonesian citizens (WNI) [1, 2].\n\nFurthermore, when a property purchase is utilized as the basis for obtaining a residency permit (such as the Second Home Visa E33A), the law distinguishes between national thresholds and local regional rules:\n*   **National Regulation:** The baseline minimum investment required for a foreigner to purchase property in Indonesia is Rp 2,000,000,000 (two billion Rupiah) [1, 2].\n*   **Local Regulation (Bali):** In Bali, local regional laws (Perda) enforce a significantly higher threshold, requiring a minimum investment of Rp 5,000,000,000 (five billion Rupiah) specifically for strata title properties [1, 2]. \n\n**Reference Files:**\n*   `nb2_faq_clienti.txt`\n*   `nb2_golden_visa.txt`",
    "conversation_id": "3e8fe6db-8873-4689-9bff-226ee875c09d",
