  "value": {
    "answer": "Under Indonesian immigration law, converting a tourist visa (Visit Stay Permit / ITK) into a Limited Stay Permit (KITAS / ITB) without leaving the country is a formal administrative procedure known as *Alih Status* (Change of Status). This mechanism is legally established by Government Regulation PP 31/2013 and updated by Permenkumham 11/2024 [1, 2].\n\nTo successfully execute an *Alih Status*, the following normative requirements and restrictions must be met:\n\n**1. Visa Eligibility (The VOA Prohibition)**\nThe most critical national requirement dictates that your current visa must be eligible for conversion. You can convert standard single-entry Visit Visas such as the C1 (Tourist) or C18 (Social) visa [3, 4]. \nHowever, it is **strictly prohibited by law** to convert a Visa on Arrival (VOA - indices B1/B4) or a Visa Exemption into a KITAS [5]. If you entered Indonesia using a VOA, *Alih Status* is legally blocked, and you are mandated to use the \"Offshore Scheme\" (exiting Indonesia to obtain a new e-Visa from abroad) [4, 6].\n\n**2. General Document Requirements (Permenkumham 11/2024, Pasal 167)**\nTo file the conversion application at the Immigration Office, you must submit:\n*   A valid National Passport containing the current valid Visa and the Entry Mark (*Tanda Masuk*) [7]. While the legal minimum validity is 6 months, a residual validity of 18 months is heavily recommended for a 1-year KITAS [8].\n*   Proof of Guarantee (*Bukti Penjaminan*) from an Indonesian Guarantor (*Penjamin*), alongside their KTP and/or Kartu Keluarga, unless you fall under a legally exempted category such as being married to an Indonesian citizen or being an eligible investor [7].\n*   Target-specific documentation depending on the KITAS category (e.g., PT PMA establishment deeds and OSS NIB for E28A Investors; foreign employment contracts and bank statements showing a minimum of USD 2,000/month for E33G Remote Workers) [8, 9].\n\n**3. The 2026 \"One Sponsor Policy\" (Crucial for Working KITAS E23)**\nIf you intend to convert your tourist visa into an E23 Working KITAS, you must comply with the newly enforced Circular SE Kemnaker No. 3/836/PK.04/I/2026. This national regulation mandates a strict \"One Sponsor Policy\": the entity that sponsored your initial Visit Visa (ITK) **must identically match** the sponsor applying for your Foreign Worker Utilization Plan (RPTKA) [10, 11]. If there is a sponsor mismatch, the *Alih Status* will be automatically rejected by the cross-agency verification system, forcing you to leave the country [11, 12].\n\n**4. National Law vs. Local Bali Enforcement**\nWhile national law permits the conversion of a C1 tourist visa to various KITAS categories (including the E33G Digital Nomad/Remote Worker visa), local enforcement in Bali (e.g., at Kantor Imigrasi Denpasar or Ngurah Rai) applies specific scrutiny. The local operational practice for converting a C1 to an E33G is classified as variable (*prassi variabile*), meaning local immigration officers evaluate these specific conversions on a strict case-by-case basis to ensure the applicant has not been conducting illegal local business while on a tourist visa [4, 13].\n\n**5. Procedural Timing and Overstay Protection**\nThe application must be submitted to the local Immigration Office before your current tourist visa expires. Under Permenkumham 11/2024 (Pasal 94A Ayat 5), as long as your application is officially registered and the immigration fees are paid prior to your visa's expiration date, you are legally protected from overstay penalties, even if the government's processing time extends beyond your visa's validity [14, 15].",
