  "value": {
    "answer": "As an expert in Indonesian company formation and PMA (Penanaman Modal Asing) investments, here is the precise regulatory framework for the minimum capital requirements for a PT PMA in 2025. These regulations have been significantly restructured following the Omnibus Law (UU Cipta Kerja No. 6 of 2023, which replaced UU 11/2020) [1-3].\n\nUnder the latest **BKPM Regulation No. 5 of 2025** (which serves as the implementing guideline for **PP No. 28 of 2025** on Risk-Based Business Licensing, replacing the older PP 5/2021), the capital requirements for a PT PMA are strictly divided into two concepts [1, 4-6]:\n\n1. **Minimum Paid-Up Capital (Modal Disetor):** The minimum paid-up capital has been formally reduced to **IDR 2.5 billion** per company [4, 7, 8]. This capital must be deposited directly into the PT PMA's corporate bank account [8, 9]. Crucially, post-Omnibus Law updates introduce a strict \"capital locking\" rule: this IDR 2.5 billion must remain in the corporate bank account for a minimum of 12 months [8-11]. It cannot be transferred back to shareholders and can only be utilized for legitimate operational and capital expenses, such as asset acquisition, construction, office rent, and employee salaries [8, 9, 11].\n2. **Total Investment Value (Total Investasi):** Despite the lowered paid-up capital, the overall investment commitment remains strictly **greater than IDR 10 billion** per 5-digit KBLI (Klasifikasi Baku Lapangan Usaha Indonesia) code and per project location [4, 7, 12]. This IDR 10 billion threshold explicitly excludes the value of land and buildings [4, 7, 12]. \n\n**KBLI Classification and PMA Status:**\nWhen structuring your investment, your capital deployment is tied directly to your chosen KBLI code. Based on the Positive Investment List (**Perpres No. 10 of 2021** and its subsequent amendments), you must always verify the foreign ownership status of your target KBLI before incorporating [4, 13]:\n*   **TERBUKA (Open):** 100% foreign ownership is permitted (e.g., KBLI 55193 for Villas, KBLI 64210 for Holding Companies) [14, 15].\n*   **TERBATAS (Restricted):** Foreign ownership is capped at a specific percentage, mandating a joint venture with a local Indonesian (WNI) partner (e.g., KBLI 73100 for Advertising is capped at 49%) [16].\n*   **TERTUTUP (Closed):** 0% foreign ownership, reserved entirely for domestic investors (PMDN) or the government (e.g., KBLI 55130 for Pondok Wisata/Homestays) [14, 17].\n\nUnder the PP 28/2025 continuous compliance framework, failure to steadily realize the IDR 10 billion investment commitment per KBLI via your quarterly LKPM (Laporan Kegiatan Penanaman Modal) reporting will trigger automated flags in the OSS system, which can result in the suspension or revocation of your business licenses [10, 18, 19].",
