  "value": {
    "answer": "For a PT PMA operating in Indonesia in 2025, the Value Added Tax (PPN) framework has undergone significant administrative and rate updates under the Harmonized Tax Law (UU HPP) and recent Ministry of Finance regulations.\n\n**VAT Rate in 2025 (PMK 131/2024)**\nWhile the statutory VAT rate officially increased to 12% on January 1, 2025, the government enacted PMK No. 131/2024 to apply this rate selectively [1]. For most standard taxable goods and services, the calculation uses an adjusted tax base (*Dasar Pengenaan Pajak* or *DPP Nilai Lain*) of 11/12 [1, 2]. This mechanism ensures that the **effective VAT rate remains at 11%** [1, 2]. The full **12% VAT rate** is strictly enforced only on deliveries of luxury goods that are subject to the Luxury Goods Sales Tax (PPnBM), such as luxury residences valued at IDR 30 billion or more, private yachts, and helicopters [1, 3]. \n\n**PKP Registration Threshold (UU KUP)**\nUnder the General Provisions and Tax Procedures Law (UU KUP), your PT PMA is legally mandated to register as a Taxable Entrepreneur (*Pengusaha Kena Pajak* or PKP) if its annual gross turnover exceeds **IDR 4.8 billion** [4]. Once this threshold is crossed, the company must register by the end of the following month [4]. However, you may opt for voluntary PKP registration before reaching this threshold [4]. For a newly established PT PMA with heavy initial capital expenditures, early registration is highly recommended so you can credit your input VAT (*Pajak Masukan*) on setup costs [4, 5].\n\n**SPDN vs. SPLN Considerations**\nAs an expert handling PT PMAs and expats, it is critical to distinguish between foreign tax residents (SPDN) and non-residents (SPLN) when dealing with VAT and withholding taxes. If your PT PMA utilizes services or intangible goods provided by an offshore foreign entity or non-resident individual (SPLN), your company must self-assess, collect, and remit the VAT (effectively 11%) on those cross-border transactions [1, 6]. Conversely, foreign directors living in Indonesia for more than 183 days or holding a KITAS/KITAP are classified as domestic tax residents (SPDN) [7]. These SPDN directors must hold the new 16-digit Personal NPWP to be legally authorized to sign corporate tax documents and electronic tax invoices on behalf of the PT PMA [8]. \n\n**CoreTax System Updates (PMK 81/2024 & PER-DJP)**\nGoverned by PMK 81/2024, the full implementation of the CoreTax Administration System (PSIAP) in 2025 has completely centralized VAT compliance [9, 10]. You must no longer use the legacy DJP Online portal or the separate desktop e-Faktur applications [10, 11]. Today, PKP registration, the generation of electronic tax invoices (*e-Faktur* as governed by DGT regulations like PER-03/PJ/2022), and the monthly filing of the VAT return (SPT Masa PPN) are fully integrated directly within the CoreTax platform [12-14]. \n\nThe deadline to file and pay your monthly SPT Masa PPN remains the end of the following month [15]. Keep in mind that CoreTax’s Compliance Risk Management (CRM) module now instantly cross-references your issued e-Fakturs with third-party data; failure to report correctly or attempting to file \"zero returns\" while actively operating will immediately trigger automated DGT scrutiny and audits [12, 16].",
