        "cited_text": "Compliance obligations CoreTax introduces several new compliance obligations that particularly affect foreign investors and multinational corporations operating in Indonesia. All businesses must ensure their registration data is current and accurate within the CoreTax system. This includes updating person-in-charge (PIC) designations, contact information, and business activity classifications. For foreign investors, this is particularly important as the system requires PIC individuals to have valid Tax Identification Numbers (NPWP) and, for foreign nationals, appropriate residence permits (KITAS). Businesses must obtain and maintain valid electronic certificates for digital document signing within the CoreTax system. This requirement extends to all tax-related transactions, including invoice generation, tax return submission, and payment processing. The system's real-time processing capabilities create new expectations for timely compliance. Businesses must adapt their internal processes to meet the system's enhanced monitoring capabilities, which can detect discrepancies and compliance issues immediately. Under the new regulations, individual taxpayers who operate businesses or practice independent professions and maintain bookkeeping are now required to withhold certain taxes, expanding the scope of withholding obligations beyond the previous system. The system's integration with banking and financial institution data creates enhanced monitoring of cross-border transactions, requiring greater attention to transfer pricing documentation and international tax compliance."
