  "value": {
    "answer": "Under *Peraturan Pemerintah (PP) Nomor 34 Tahun 2021*, the RPTKA (Foreign Worker Utilization Plan) approval functions directly as the formal work authorization, effectively absorbing and replacing the abolished physical IMTA document [1, 2]. \n\nTo sponsor a foreign worker (TKA) for an E23 Working KITAS, a PT PMA must satisfy a series of strict national requirements through the Ministry of Manpower's (Kemnaker) Molina portal before the e-Visa can be issued [2, 3]. While local immigration offices in Bali handle the final biometric capture and physical KITAS issuance, the RPTKA prerequisites are governed uniformly at the national level.\n\nThe PT PMA must fulfill the following mandatory requirements:\n\n**1. Corporate Compliance and Cross-Agency Verification**\nThe PT PMA must prove its legal and operational standing by submitting its Deed of Establishment alongside the Ministerial Approval (*SK Kemenkumham*) and its Company Tax ID (NPWP) [4, 5]. The Kemnaker system now cross-checks compliance with other agencies, meaning the PT PMA must have:\n*   An active NIB (Nomor Induk Berusaha) in the OSS system [3, 6].\n*   An up-to-date Mandatory Company Employment Report (WLKP) [4, 7].\n*   Active registration and contribution to the national social security system (BPJS Ketenagakerjaan) for its local Indonesian employees [4, 5, 7].\n\n**2. Sector (KBLI) and Position (Jabatan) Eligibility**\nThe specific job title requested for the foreign worker must strictly correspond to the PT PMA's KBLI business sector code [8, 9]. \n*   **Permitted Roles:** The position must be explicitly listed within the 20 allowed business sectors governed by *Kepmenaker Nomor 228 Tahun 2019* [10-12]. \n*   **Prohibited Roles:** The PT PMA is absolutely forbidden from placing the foreign worker in any of the 19 specifically restricted Human Resources and Industrial Relations positions, as dictated by *Kepmenaker Nomor 349 Tahun 2019* [13-15].\n\n**3. Organizational and Transfer-of-Knowledge Requirements**\nThe PT PMA must submit a formal organizational chart (*Bagan organisasi*) demonstrating the exact hierarchical placement of the foreign worker [4, 8, 16]. Furthermore, the company must sign a formal commitment (*Surat pernyataan pendampingan*) to appoint a local Indonesian companion worker (*TKI Pendamping*) who will shadow the foreign worker to ensure the transfer of skills and technology [4, 17, 18].\n\n**4. Payment of the DKP-TKA Fund**\nOnce the RPTKA is approved, the PT PMA is legally obligated to pay the *Dana Kompensasi Penggunaan TKA* (DKP-TKA) [1, 19]. This compensation fund is fixed at USD 100 per position for every month of the contract [19, 20]. The full amount for the entire contract duration must be paid in advance; failing to do so will block the issuance of the E23 Visa [21, 22].\n\n**5. The 2026 \"One Sponsor Policy\" (For Onshore Conversions)**\nIf the PT PMA is attempting to hire a foreigner who is already inside Indonesia via an onshore status conversion (*Alih Status*), it must comply with *Surat Edaran Kemnaker No. 3/836/PK.04/I/2026* [23, 24]. This policy mandates that the corporate sponsor listed on the foreigner's current Visit Stay Permit (ITK) must identically match the PT PMA applying for the RPTKA [23, 24]. If the sponsors do not match, the application is automatically rejected, and the PT PMA must process the worker via the \"Offshore Scheme,\" requiring the foreigner to leave Indonesia [23, 25].",
