    "answer": "The company acting as the employer—such as a PT PMA—is strictly responsible for its sponsor obligations, which encompass managing the RPTKA (Foreign Worker Utilization Plan) and the associated DKPTKA procedures [1]. \n\n*Please note that the specific fee amount and the historical term \"DPKK\" are not explicitly detailed in the provided sources. You may want to independently verify the following information:* The term DPKK (*Dana Pengembangan Keahlian dan Keterampilan*) has been updated in modern Indonesian employment regulations to **DKPTKA** (*Dana Kompensasi Penggunaan Tenaga Kerja Asing*). The DKPTKA is a mandatory compensation fund set at USD 100 per month for each foreign worker employed. By law, this fee must be paid entirely by the employer (the PT PMA) to the Ministry of Manpower to fund local workforce training, and it cannot be deducted from the foreign employee's salary.\n\n**Regulatory Context & Post-Omnibus Law Updates**\nFollowing the Omnibus Law (UU No. 6 of 2023 on Job Creation) and its implementing regulation for Risk-Based Business Licensing (PP No. 28 of 2025), the procedures for employing foreign workers have been heavily integrated into the national digital infrastructure. If a PT PMA plans to hire foreign workers (TKA), it must submit its RPTKA through the electronic system managed by the Ministry of Manpower [2, 3]. \n\nOnce the Ministry of Manpower validates and approves the RPTKA, this authorization is automatically forwarded to the OSS system and the immigration authorities [2, 4]. Under these post-Omnibus Law rules, the PT PMA serves as the legal sponsor and must handle all corporate-side prerequisites (including the DKPTKA compensation fund) before the actual work permit and limited stay visa (ITAS) can be issued [1].\n\n**KBLI & PMA Status Applicability**\nWhile the DKPTKA obligation applies to any corporate entity hiring foreign workers, a PT PMA must first ensure it is legally established under a KBLI code that permits foreign investment according to the Positive Investment List (Perpres No. 10 of 2021) [5]. For example, if you establish a Business Consulting firm under **KBLI 70201** (which is classified as **TERBUKA** – 100% open to foreign ownership), the PT PMA can legally sponsor foreign consultants, provided it fulfills the RPTKA submission and the subsequent DKPTKA payment obligations [6]. Conversely, if you operate in a restricted sector (e.g., KBLI 73100 Advertising, classified as **TERBATAS** with max 49% foreign ownership), the same strict DKPTKA employer obligations apply to any expatriate staff you hire [5, 7].",
