  "value": {
    "answer": "Transfer pricing rules for a PT PMA engaging in cross-border transactions with its foreign parent company are strictly governed by the Arm's Length Principle (ALP) under Article 18 of the Income Tax Law and the newly consolidated **PMK No. 172 of 2023** (which replaced previous regulations like PMK 213/2016) [1, 2]. As your tax consultant, I must emphasize that compliance with the ALP is mandatory to ensure profits are reported where economic value is genuinely created, regardless of whether you meet documentation thresholds [3, 4].\n\n**Documentation Thresholds**\nYour PT PMA is legally required to prepare and maintain formal Transfer Pricing Documentation (TP Doc)—consisting of a Master File and a Local File—if your operations in the preceding tax year met any of the following thresholds:\n*   Gross turnover exceeds IDR 50 billion [5, 6].\n*   Related-party transactions involving tangible goods exceed IDR 20 billion [5].\n*   Related-party transactions involving service provisions, interest payments, or intangibles exceed IDR 5 billion [5, 7].\nFurthermore, if your multinational group's consolidated gross revenue reaches IDR 11 trillion, the parent entity or the designated PT PMA must submit a Country-by-Country Report (CbCR) [8, 9].\n\n**Preliminary Analysis and SPDN vs. SPLN Implications**\nPMK 172/2023 introduces a strict requirement to conduct a mandatory \"preliminary analysis\" before applying transfer pricing methods to specific transactions, such as intra-group services, financial loans, and royalties [10-12]. You must document the commercial justification and prove that the PT PMA received actual economic benefits from the foreign parent [10, 13, 14].\n\nIn managing these transactions, we must strictly distinguish between your PT PMA, which is a Domestic Tax Resident (SPDN), and your foreign parent company, which is a Foreign Tax Subject (SPLN). If the Directorate General of Taxes (DJP) determines that your intercompany pricing deviates from the ALP, they will issue a primary adjustment to your PT PMA's taxable income [15, 16]. Consequently, a secondary adjustment will automatically reclassify the excess profit as a constructive dividend distributed to the SPLN parent [15-17]. \n\nThis reclassification immediately triggers a PPh 26 withholding tax liability of 20% on the deemed dividend [15, 18]. To reduce this PPh 26 rate using a Tax Treaty (P3B), the SPLN parent cannot simply rely on a Certificate of Domicile (CoD); under the new **PMK 112/2025**, the SPLN must pass rigorous anti-abuse tests, including the Principal Purpose Test (PPT) and Beneficial Ownership requirements, to prove the transaction's economic substance [19-21].\n\n**CoreTax System (PSIAP) and KUP Audits**\nYou must be highly vigilant regarding the new CoreTax administration system implemented under **PMK 81/2024** [22-24]. The CoreTax Compliance Risk Management (CRM) module continuously cross-references your PT PMA’s intercompany transaction volumes, corporate tax returns (SPT Tahunan), and withholding tax data in real-time [25-27]. \n\nUnder the General Provisions and Tax Procedures Law (UU KUP No. 28/2007, as amended), the DJP retains a five-year statute of limitations to issue transfer pricing assessments and levy heavy administrative penalties [15, 24, 28]. Because the CoreTax system automatically flags anomalies—such as low effective tax rates compared to local peers or high-volume transactions with lower-tax jurisdictions—ensuring your Local and Master Files are meticulously prepared and aligned with your digital filings is critical to surviving a DJP audit [15, 27].",
