    "answer": "As an expert in Indonesian immigration law (2024-2026), I can clarify the fundamental legal and operational differences between the KITAS E23 and KITAS E31 indices, based on the national regulatory framework and local enforcement practices in Bali.\n\n**1. Purpose and Permitted Activities**\n*   **KITAS E23 (Working KITAS):** This Limited Stay Permit is strictly for foreign workers (Tenaga Kerja Asing or TKA) employed by an Indonesian corporate entity (such as a PT or PT PMA). Governed by *Permenkumham No. 11 Tahun 2024, Pasal 33 ayat (2) huruf b*, it legally authorizes the foreigner to work in Indonesia [1]. However, the foreigner is strictly restricted to working for their specific sponsor in an approved job title (jabatan) that identically matches the company's business sector (KBLI) [2, 3]. \n*   **KITAS E31 (Family/Dependent KITAS):** This permit is exclusively for family reunification (Penyatuan Keluarga). Under *Permenkumham No. 11 Tahun 2024, Pasal 33 ayat (2) huruf h*, it is granted to the spouse or unmarried children under 18 of an Indonesian citizen (WNI) or a resident holding a valid KITAS/KITAP [4-6]. **Critical restriction:** Holders of the E31 permit are strictly prohibited from working or generating any income in Indonesia without obtaining a separate work permit [7, 8].\n\n**2. Sponsorship (Penjamin) Requirements**\n*   **KITAS E23:** The employing Indonesian company acts as the mandatory formal guarantor (*penjamin*) under *Permenimipas No. 5 Tahun 2025* [9]. Furthermore, under the national 2026 \"One Sponsor Policy\" (*Surat Edaran Kemnaker No. 3/836/PK.04/I/2026*), any onshore status conversion (Alih Status) requires that the corporate sponsor on the foreigner's initial Visit Permit identically matches the new RPTKA sponsor; otherwise, the application is automatically rejected [10, 11].\n*   **KITAS E31:** \n    *   If the principal sponsor is a foreign KITAS holder (e.g., holding an E23), the principal's company or the principal themselves assumes responsibility as the guarantor for the dependents [12].\n    *   If the principal sponsor is an Indonesian citizen (WNI), under *Permenimipas No. 5 Tahun 2025* and *Permenkumham No. 11 Tahun 2024 Pasal 191*, the foreign spouse is legally **exempted** from the obligation to have a formal guarantor. The WNI spouse simply acts as the \"Penanggung Jawab\" (Responsible Person) [13-15].\n\n**3. Ministry Approvals and Financial Obligations**\n*   **KITAS E23:** Requires a complex, multi-agency procedure governed by *Peraturan Pemerintah (PP) No. 34 Tahun 2021* [16, 17]. The sponsoring company must obtain an approved Foreign Worker Utilization Plan (RPTKA) from the Ministry of Manpower (Kemnaker) [18] and must pay the mandatory DKP-TKA compensation fund of USD 100 per position per month in advance [19].\n*   **KITAS E31:** Does not require Kemnaker approval, an RPTKA, or DKP-TKA payments [20]. It primarily requires civil documents (such as apostilled and translated marriage or birth certificates) and proof of adequate living funds to support the family [20, 21].\n\n**4. National Law vs. Local Bali Enforcement**\nWhile the regulations for both visas are established at the national level by the Directorate General of Immigration (under Kemenimipas) and Kemnaker [22, 23], local processing at offices like *Kantor Imigrasi Kelas I Khusus TPI Ngurah Rai* in Bali presents operational differences. For an E31 family permit, an onshore conversion (Alih Status) locally takes an average of 7 to 14 working days once documents are submitted [24, 25]. Conversely, the E23 requires the national RPTKA approval from Jakarta first (which takes several weeks) before the local Bali immigration office can execute the biometric capture and issue the physical stay permit [26, 27].",
